            <ArticleTitle>Spinal cord stimulation for the treatment of angina and peripheral vascular disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>18-23</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spinal cord stimulation has been shown to be an effective and well-established treatment for failed back surgery syndrome, complex regional pain syndrome, and other neuropathic pain states. Recent advances in this therapy have led to its use in enhancing blood flow and reducing ischemic pain patterns. The application of spinal cord stimulation to treat angina and improve outcomes in patients suffering from peripheral vascular disease is now becoming a part of the algorithmic standard of care. This article examines the selection of patients, application of the therapy, outcomes, and future uses of stimulation for patients afflicted with these diseases. This article also examines possible study protocols to further examine the overall outcome of these therapies.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
